    {
        "content": "<p>I had a chance to look at this now <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"908200\">@Anvit Aggarwal</span>. The reason I chose the universes is like that is that, in principle, all nodes could be described by _finite powers of the type <code>β</code>_. From the perspective of mathematical logic, universes \"should\" be a statement only about cardinalities; if it's finite (or countable), it can always be placed in <code>Type</code>. By making nodes live in the same universe as <code>β</code>, in principle if you wanted to write down a branching program where <code>β</code> is a very large type, the nodes have the correct universe to hold that much data. You could imagine for instance a branching program where each variable is a real number, and we have an uncountable number of nodes at each layer.</p>\n<p>While this reasoning about cardinalities doesn't always align with what's practical from a software/ergonomics standpoint, it's _typically_ quite well-aligned: if you're doing the operations you \"should\" do with your data, the kind that keep universes straight, then the cardinalities also combine in the ways they should.</p>",
        "id": 582652429,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1774903701
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I understand your gripe here. For a monoid program, it's natural to label the nodes by the output type γ, so you want the nodes to live in that universe. And then it goes kaput</p>",
        "id": 582652654,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1774903809
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>When do we encounter uncountably infinite branching in monoid programs?</p>",
        "id": 582652746,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1774903858
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess my recommendation would be to just take <code>γ : Type</code> as well, so that it also lives in the same universe as <code>β</code>. Now everything is essentially working out fine, and you have no universe problems to worry about. Then, if you /really/ wanted to extend it to see it handle larger universes, you can always just lower <code>γ : Type w</code> to be just the fragment of the monoid generated by the generators used in your program. That gives you the countable cardinality that you need to lower it to Type again and so you can reduce to that case - if you really want to.</p>",
        "id": 582652912,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1774903934
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"466334\">Shreyas Srinivas</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/513188-CSLib/topic/Formalizing.20Monoid.20Programs.20in.20CircuitComp.2FCSLib/near/582652746\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>When do we encounter uncountably infinite branching in monoid programs?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>In monoid programs? Never, as far as I'm aware. But the definition in question here isn't about monoid programs, it's about general branching programs, which I absolutely have seen with infinite branching.</p>\n<p>For that matter, I've never seen a monoid program that needed the monoid to live in a higher von Neumann universe.</p>",
        "id": 582653088,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1774904008
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"448405\">@Alex Meiburg</span>  and others,</p>\n<p>I wanted to share an update on this project. I’ve added an modified version of toBranchingProgram' (supporting arbitrary accepting sets F). I have also proved that toBranchingProgram' computes the same language as the original group/Barrington program.</p>\n<p>Summary of progress so far:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The main result is the statement exists_boundedWidthBP_poly_iff_exists_monoidProgram.</li>\n<li>I’m currently working on the reverse (monoid program ⇒ BP) direction.</li>\n<li>The key result so far is the lemma toBranchingProgram_computes, which shows that if a program family PF computes a function family A, then (PF n).toBranchingProgram' computes A as well.</li>\n<li>The main helper lemma is toBranchingProgram'_evalLayer, showing that evaluation at each layer matches between the Barrington program and the corresponding LayeredBranchingProgram.</li>\n<li>I also defined <code>evalLayer</code> for Barrington programs as well to handle partial evaluation after any number of steps.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>As for next steps, 1) to show that toBranchingProgram' returns a bounded width and polynomial size BranchingProgram, and 2) proving the forward direction.</p>\n<p>Code: <a href=\"https://github.com/Timeroot/CircuitComp/compare/main...AnvitAggarwal:CircuitComp:main\">Fork of CircuitComp</a></p>\n<p>I’d appreciate any feedback. And any suggestions for the next steps would be great.</p>\n<p>Thank you!</p>",
        "id": 594926546,
        "sender_full_name": "Anvit Aggarwal",
        "timestamp": 1778696618
    }
]